## Key Observations & Notes

- **Basis of all reported figures:** All IRDAI "NL" forms are statutory **IGAAP** filings only — no IFRS figures are published anywhere in these disclosures, hence Profit After Tax (IFRS) and Claims ratio (IFRS) rows had no value and were omitted from the table above.
- **GWP basis:** The Gross Direct Premium figures in NL-4/NL-35/NL-36 are reported on the statutory (1/n applicable) basis consistently across all four quarters — no ex-1/n alternative figure is shown, so no basis-mixing issue arises.
- **Retail vs. Group Health split:** The disclosures break premium only by **Health / Personal Accident / Travel**, not by Retail vs. Group within Health — hence Retail health GWP could not be populated and was excluded per the "leave blank rather than estimate" rule.
- **FY26 PAT of ₹12.16 cr** is markedly lower than FY25's ₹155.18 cr (per the same "Upto Quarter" comparative column), driven by a sharp deterioration in the underwriting result — Combined Ratio worsened from **103%** (FY25) to **107%** (FY26), with the claims ratio rising from **65%** to **70%**.
- **Solvency ratio of 1.68x** is comfortably above the IRDAI-mandated minimum of 1.50x, though flat year-on-year versus FY25's same metric — indicating capital adequacy is being maintained but not expanding, despite the ₹10,000 lakh debenture issuance during the year (per NL-11 Borrowings Schedule).
- **Retail health market share and Investment yield** are not disclosed anywhere in the IRDAI public-disclosure format or on the index page <doc_source>step3web5</doc_source>. These metrics would need to be sourced from a separate investor presentation or annual report, which was not part of the specified document set — I recommend checking Care Health Insurance's Q4 FY26 investor presentation (if available) or its FY25-26 Annual Report for these two data points, as they are commonly disclosed there rather than in statutory NL filings.
- **Investment yield distinction:** NL-20 discloses only an "Investment income ratio" of **0.07** (investment income to NEP basis), which is a materially different metric from the conventional "yield on investments" (investment income/average AUM). To avoid misrepresenting the data, this was left blank rather than substituting a non-equivalent ratio.</ans>
